1
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:08,465
A quantum computer could crack the codes that keep the internet's secrets.

2
00:00:08,465 --> 00:00:10,773
This is the story of how:

3
00:00:10,773 --> 00:00:14,235
Shor's algorithm, the quantum Fourier transform at its heart,

4
00:00:14,235 --> 00:00:19,235
and the race to keep fragile qubits alive long enough to run it.

5
00:00:22,317 --> 00:00:24,240
This arrow is a qubit.

6
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:27,317
Straight up means zero, straight down means one,

7
00:00:27,317 --> 00:00:30,009
and anywhere in between is a superposition:

8
00:00:30,009 --> 00:00:32,317
a blend of both at once.

9
00:00:33,017 --> 00:00:38,401
The blend is described by two amplitudes, one for zero and one for one.

10
00:00:38,401 --> 00:00:42,247
Square them, and you get the odds of measuring each.

11
00:00:42,917 --> 00:00:45,994
But an amplitude is more than a size.

12
00:00:45,994 --> 00:00:51,378
It also points in a direction, its phase, like the hand of a clock.

13
00:00:51,378 --> 00:00:56,763
Turn that hand, and the odds of a measurement do not change at all.

14
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:59,373
Phase matters when amplitudes meet.

15
00:00:59,373 --> 00:01:01,681
A Hadamard gate adds them up:

16
00:01:01,681 --> 00:01:05,142
hands pointing the same way reinforce, opposite hands cancel.

17
00:01:05,142 --> 00:01:10,912
That is interference, and it turns a hidden phase into an answer you can measure.

18
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:15,873
One qubit holds two amplitudes.

19
00:01:15,873 --> 00:01:18,950
Two qubits are not just two separate arrows:

20
00:01:18,950 --> 00:01:24,334
the pair has four possibilities, zero zero, zero one, one zero and one one,

21
00:01:24,334 --> 00:01:26,257
and an amplitude for each.

22
00:01:26,950 --> 00:01:29,642
Every qubit you add doubles that list.

23
00:01:29,642 --> 00:01:32,334
Ten qubits hold over a thousand amplitudes.

24
00:01:32,334 --> 00:01:36,565
Three hundred hold more than there are atoms in the universe.

25
00:01:36,565 --> 00:01:40,411
A register of qubits can hold every number at once.

26
00:01:41,083 --> 00:01:42,621
Now the strange part.

27
00:01:42,621 --> 00:01:48,006
A Hadamard, then a CNOT, leaves amplitude only on zero zero and one one.

28
00:01:48,006 --> 00:01:50,314
Measure the pair a hundred times:

29
00:01:50,314 --> 00:01:54,929
each qubit alone is a coin flip, but the two always agree.

30
00:01:55,616 --> 00:01:59,078
This pair cannot be split into two separate arrows.

31
00:01:59,078 --> 00:02:01,770
Each qubit's own arrow shrinks to nothing,

32
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:04,847
because the information lives only in the correlation.

33
00:02:04,847 --> 00:02:09,847
That is entanglement, and both Shor's algorithm and error correction depend on it.

34
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:16,319
Line up a few qubits, and their clocks can wind around together, like a wave.

35
00:02:16,319 --> 00:02:20,550
Waves of phase are the language of the quantum Fourier transform,

36
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:22,473
the engine of Shor's algorithm.

37
00:02:25,516 --> 00:02:27,439
Multiplying two primes is easy.

38
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:29,362
Undoing the multiplication is not.

39
00:02:29,362 --> 00:02:34,747
RSA, the encryption that protects much of the internet, is built on that gap.

40
00:02:35,416 --> 00:02:37,724
Shor's idea starts with something simple.

41
00:02:37,724 --> 00:02:43,878
Pick a number a, and take its powers, keeping only the remainder after dividing by N.

42
00:02:43,878 --> 00:02:46,955
For N equals fifteen and a equals seven:

43
00:02:46,955 --> 00:02:50,032
one, seven, four, thirteen, and then one again.

44
00:02:50,816 --> 00:02:53,509
The pattern repeats, forever, every four steps.

45
00:02:53,509 --> 00:02:55,432
Call that the period, r.

46
00:02:56,116 --> 00:02:59,193
Once you know r, the factors fall out.

47
00:02:59,193 --> 00:03:03,424
Seven to the power two is forty nine, which leaves four.

48
00:03:03,424 --> 00:03:09,578
Four minus one is three, four plus one is five, and fifteen is three times five.

49
00:03:10,250 --> 00:03:16,404
For a number hundreds of digits long, the period can be hundreds of digits long too.

50
00:03:16,404 --> 00:03:19,096
No computer could ever walk that far.

51
00:03:19,096 --> 00:03:21,404
A quantum computer does not walk.

52
00:03:21,404 --> 00:03:23,327
It listens for the rhythm.

53
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:30,229
Listening for a rhythm is what the Fourier transform does.

54
00:03:30,229 --> 00:03:36,768
Feed it a signal that repeats, and it answers with a sharp spike at the repetition rate.

55
00:03:37,450 --> 00:03:39,757
The quantum version works on amplitudes.

56
00:03:39,757 --> 00:03:44,757
Give it a single number x, and it returns every value at once,

57
00:03:44,757 --> 00:03:48,604
with value y's clock turned x times y over N.

58
00:03:48,604 --> 00:03:53,219
The clocks wind around x times, a wave whose speed encodes x.

59
00:03:53,916 --> 00:03:55,839
And it is astonishingly cheap.

60
00:03:55,839 --> 00:04:00,070
A Hadamard on each qubit, then smaller and smaller controlled turns:

61
00:04:00,070 --> 00:04:02,378
a half, a quarter, an eighth.

62
00:04:02,378 --> 00:04:06,609
Each qubit becomes one clock hand, spinning at its own speed.

63
00:04:08,316 --> 00:04:10,239
Now feed it a rhythm:

64
00:04:10,239 --> 00:04:12,547
equal amplitude on every fourth value.

65
00:04:12,547 --> 00:04:17,547
Each output adds up one clock hand from every tooth of the comb.

66
00:04:17,547 --> 00:04:20,239
Where the hands agree, they add up.

67
00:04:20,239 --> 00:04:21,778
Everywhere else they cancel.

68
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:25,911
Four sharp peaks, at multiples of sixteen over four.

69
00:04:25,911 --> 00:04:28,219
Their spacing gives away the period.

70
00:04:28,219 --> 00:04:34,373
When the period does not divide evenly, the peaks blur, but they still point the way.

71
00:04:37,416 --> 00:04:41,262
Here is the whole algorithm, simulated exactly, factoring twenty one.

72
00:04:41,262 --> 00:04:42,031
Two registers:

73
00:04:42,031 --> 00:04:47,416
one counts x, the other will hold two to the x, mod twenty one.

74
00:04:48,116 --> 00:04:53,885
Hadamards put every x, from zero to one hundred twenty seven, into superposition at once.

75
00:04:54,583 --> 00:05:01,121
One big circuit computes two to the x, mod twenty one, for all of them in parallel.

76
00:05:01,121 --> 00:05:04,198
Each x is now entangled with its value.

77
00:05:04,883 --> 00:05:07,960
We measure the output register and see eight.

78
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:11,037
Every x that does not give eight vanishes.

79
00:05:11,037 --> 00:05:14,883
What survives is a comb, with teeth exactly six apart.

80
00:05:15,583 --> 00:05:22,506
The quantum Fourier transform turns that comb into peaks near multiples of one hundred twenty eight over six.

81
00:05:23,183 --> 00:05:25,490
Measure, and we get twenty one.

82
00:05:25,490 --> 00:05:30,490
Twenty one over one hundred twenty eight is very close to one sixth.

83
00:05:30,490 --> 00:05:35,106
A continued fraction finds that fraction, and its six is the period.

84
00:05:35,783 --> 00:05:38,090
Two to the three is eight.

85
00:05:38,090 --> 00:05:41,937
Seven and nine each share a factor with twenty one:

86
00:05:41,937 --> 00:05:43,090
seven, and three.

87
00:05:43,090 --> 00:05:45,398
Twenty one is three times seven.

88
00:05:48,449 --> 00:05:50,372
That was a perfect machine.

89
00:05:50,372 --> 00:05:51,911
Real qubits make mistakes.

90
00:05:51,911 --> 00:05:57,680
So let every qubit, after every step, have a small chance of a random error,

91
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:01,142
and run the circuit for fifteen two hundred times.

92
00:06:01,816 --> 00:06:06,816
At one error in a thousand, most runs still land on a peak.

93
00:06:06,816 --> 00:06:10,278
At one in a hundred, most of them miss.

94
00:06:10,278 --> 00:06:13,739
At five in a hundred, the peaks are gone,

95
00:06:13,739 --> 00:06:17,585
and the quantum computer has become a random number generator.

96
00:06:18,283 --> 00:06:20,206
And this circuit was tiny.

97
00:06:20,206 --> 00:06:24,437
Breaking real encryption takes around a million qubits running for days.

98
00:06:24,437 --> 00:06:30,590
To finish without a single error, each operation could fail only about once in a trillion.

99
00:06:30,590 --> 00:06:34,437
The best qubits today fail about once in a thousand.

100
00:06:35,116 --> 00:06:37,424
No physical qubit is that good.

101
00:06:37,424 --> 00:06:41,270
So we build better ones out of many imperfect ones.

102
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:46,270
That is quantum error correction, and it is the rest of this story.

103
00:06:49,316 --> 00:06:50,854
Back to our arrow.

104
00:06:50,854 --> 00:06:55,470
Up is zero, down is one, and Shor's algorithm lives in between,

105
00:06:55,470 --> 00:07:00,470
in the amplitudes and their phases, on every qubit, for the whole computation.

106
00:07:01,183 --> 00:07:04,644
The trouble is that the arrow never holds still.

107
00:07:04,644 --> 00:07:08,490
Stray fields, heat, a control pulse meant for a neighbor:

108
00:07:08,490 --> 00:07:12,721
each one nudges it a little, and the nudges add up.

109
00:07:13,449 --> 00:07:17,296
On an ordinary computer we would fight this with backups.

110
00:07:17,296 --> 00:07:20,757
Copy the bit three times and take a vote.

111
00:07:20,757 --> 00:07:23,065
For qubits, that move is forbidden.

112
00:07:23,749 --> 00:07:30,288
Try to copy an unknown state with the obvious circuit, and you do not get two copies.

113
00:07:30,288 --> 00:07:32,596
You get two qubits tangled together,

114
00:07:32,596 --> 00:07:37,211
and their overlap with the copies you wanted is exactly one half.

115
00:07:37,883 --> 00:07:39,806
This is the no-cloning theorem.

116
00:07:39,806 --> 00:07:43,267
Whatever protects a qubit, it cannot be a backup.

117
00:07:46,316 --> 00:07:47,854
Here is the trick.

118
00:07:47,854 --> 00:07:51,316
Instead of copying the state, we spread it out.

119
00:07:51,316 --> 00:07:56,701
Two CNOT gates turn alpha zero plus beta one into alpha zero zero zero,

120
00:07:56,701 --> 00:07:58,624
plus beta one one one.

121
00:07:59,316 --> 00:08:06,239
Look at any one of these qubits on its own, and its arrow has shrunk toward the center.

122
00:08:06,239 --> 00:08:09,701
The information is not in any single qubit anymore.

123
00:08:09,701 --> 00:08:12,008
It lives in how they relate.

124
00:08:12,683 --> 00:08:15,375
Now suppose noise flips one of them.

125
00:08:15,375 --> 00:08:19,606
We cannot simply look, because measuring would destroy alpha and beta.

126
00:08:19,606 --> 00:08:21,913
So we ask a gentler question:

127
00:08:21,913 --> 00:08:23,452
do these two agree?

128
00:08:24,149 --> 00:08:27,611
A helper qubit collects the parity of each pair.

129
00:08:27,611 --> 00:08:33,380
Two checks give two bits, the syndrome, and the syndrome points straight at the culprit.

130
00:08:34,049 --> 00:08:37,511
Flip it back, and the state is restored perfectly,

131
00:08:37,511 --> 00:08:40,203
without our ever learning alpha or beta.

132
00:08:40,203 --> 00:08:45,203
That is a stabilizer measurement, the heart of every quantum error correcting code.

133
00:08:48,249 --> 00:08:50,942
But real errors are not clean flips.

134
00:08:50,942 --> 00:08:55,557
They are tiny rotations, a sliver of a turn at a time.

135
00:08:55,557 --> 00:08:59,403
How can a code built for flips fix a drift?

136
00:09:00,083 --> 00:09:06,237
Let us rotate one qubit by a small angle, run the checks, and see what happens.

137
00:09:06,237 --> 00:09:07,775
Then do it again.

138
00:09:07,775 --> 00:09:08,929
Two hundred times.

139
00:09:09,616 --> 00:09:11,539
Only two things ever happen.

140
00:09:11,539 --> 00:09:16,924
Most of the time the checks report nothing, and the drift is simply gone.

141
00:09:16,924 --> 00:09:22,308
Now and then they report a full flip, which we know how to undo.

142
00:09:22,983 --> 00:09:29,137
The odds are cosine squared and sine squared of theta over two, right on the formula.

143
00:09:29,137 --> 00:09:33,367
Measuring the checks forces the error to make up its mind.

144
00:09:33,367 --> 00:09:36,060
A continuous problem becomes a discrete one,

145
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:40,290
and that is why quantum error correction can work at all.

146
00:09:43,349 --> 00:09:46,426
There is a second kind of error, though.

147
00:09:46,426 --> 00:09:52,580
A phase flip leaves every bit where it was, and only changes the sign between them.

148
00:09:52,580 --> 00:09:55,657
Our parity checks cannot see it at all.

149
00:09:56,349 --> 00:10:02,888
The fix is to also check in a rotated basis, where phase flips look like bit flips.

150
00:10:02,888 --> 00:10:08,272
Do both, everywhere, on a two dimensional grid, and you get the surface code.

151
00:10:08,949 --> 00:10:10,872
White circles are data qubits.

152
00:10:10,872 --> 00:10:13,180
Teal squares check for bit flips.

153
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:15,488
Violet squares check for phase flips.

154
00:10:15,488 --> 00:10:19,334
Each check touches four neighbors, or two along the edge.

155
00:10:20,016 --> 00:10:23,862
Flip one qubit and the checks beside it light up.

156
00:10:23,862 --> 00:10:28,093
Flip a whole chain, and only its two ends light up.

157
00:10:28,093 --> 00:10:30,401
Everything in between cancels in pairs.

158
00:10:31,083 --> 00:10:33,006
And that is the catch.

159
00:10:33,006 --> 00:10:38,006
A chain from one edge to the other lights up nothing at all,

160
00:10:38,006 --> 00:10:40,313
yet it flips the stored information.

161
00:10:40,313 --> 00:10:44,929
The shortest such chain has length d, the distance of the code.

162
00:10:47,982 --> 00:10:52,598
So here is the puzzle a quantum computer faces, over and over.

163
00:10:52,598 --> 00:10:54,905
Noise strikes somewhere we cannot see.

164
00:10:54,905 --> 00:10:57,598
All we get are the lit checks.

165
00:10:58,316 --> 00:11:02,162
Each lit check is the end of some error chain.

166
00:11:02,162 --> 00:11:08,316
To explain them, we have to pair them up, or send them off to the boundary.

167
00:11:09,016 --> 00:11:12,477
Strip away the qubits and keep only what matters.

168
00:11:12,477 --> 00:11:19,016
Checks become points, and the cost of joining two is the number of errors it would take.

169
00:11:19,716 --> 00:11:23,177
Short chains are far more likely than long ones,

170
00:11:23,177 --> 00:11:27,793
so the best guess is the pairing with the smallest total length.

171
00:11:27,793 --> 00:11:30,100
That is minimum weight perfect matching,

172
00:11:30,100 --> 00:11:32,793
and Edmonds' blossom algorithm finds it efficiently.

173
00:11:33,516 --> 00:11:37,746
Fold the graph back onto the grid and apply the corrections.

174
00:11:37,746 --> 00:11:41,593
Together with the hidden errors, they close into harmless loops.

175
00:11:41,593 --> 00:11:43,131
The qubit is saved.

176
00:11:43,849 --> 00:11:45,003
But not always.

177
00:11:45,003 --> 00:11:49,618
Push the noise higher, and the most likely explanation can be wrong.

178
00:11:49,618 --> 00:11:56,157
The fix and the errors join into a chain across the grid, and the information is lost.

179
00:11:59,248 --> 00:12:01,555
Real hardware adds one more wrinkle.

180
00:12:01,555 --> 00:12:03,478
The checks themselves are noisy.

181
00:12:03,478 --> 00:12:06,555
Sometimes a check simply reports the wrong answer.

182
00:12:07,248 --> 00:12:10,325
So we measure every check again and again,

183
00:12:10,325 --> 00:12:14,555
and stack the rounds into a block of space and time.

184
00:12:14,555 --> 00:12:16,863
What we look for is change:

185
00:12:16,863 --> 00:12:20,325
a check whose answer differs from the round before.

186
00:12:21,014 --> 00:12:26,014
A real qubit error shows up as a pair in the same round.

187
00:12:26,014 --> 00:12:31,399
A check that lied shows up as the same check, in two consecutive rounds.

188
00:12:32,081 --> 00:12:36,312
Decoding becomes matching in three dimensions, through space and through time.

189
00:12:36,312 --> 00:12:37,850
Same algorithm, bigger graph.

190
00:12:40,948 --> 00:12:43,640
Here is the question that decides everything.

191
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:48,640
If we build a bigger code, do we get fewer errors, or more?

192
00:12:49,314 --> 00:12:52,776
More qubits means more places for noise to strike.

193
00:12:52,776 --> 00:12:57,007
But a bigger code also needs a longer chain to fail.

194
00:12:57,007 --> 00:13:00,853
Which effect wins depends on how noisy each qubit is.

195
00:13:01,548 --> 00:13:06,548
Each point on these curves is thousands of simulated memories, every one decoded.

196
00:13:06,548 --> 00:13:10,394
Above a critical noise level, the bigger codes do worse.

197
00:13:10,394 --> 00:13:14,240
Below it, every step up in size suppresses errors further.

198
00:13:14,914 --> 00:13:16,837
That crossing is the threshold.

199
00:13:16,837 --> 00:13:20,299
For this simple noise it sits near ten percent.

200
00:13:20,299 --> 00:13:22,607
With checks that lie, near three.

201
00:13:22,607 --> 00:13:26,068
For full circuits with faulty gates, closer to one.

202
00:13:26,748 --> 00:13:27,517
Below it,

203
00:13:27,517 --> 00:13:33,671
each increase of the distance by two divides the error rate by a factor called lambda.

204
00:13:33,671 --> 00:13:37,517
In 2024, Google's Willow chip measured lambda of about two:

205
00:13:37,517 --> 00:13:40,209
the first convincing quantum memory below threshold.

206
00:13:43,246 --> 00:13:48,630
There is one more constraint, and it is the one this project is about:

207
00:13:48,630 --> 00:13:49,015
time.

208
00:13:49,712 --> 00:13:55,097
A superconducting chip produces a fresh round of syndrome data about once every microsecond.

209
00:13:55,097 --> 00:13:57,405
The decoder has to keep up.

210
00:13:58,112 --> 00:14:00,420
Exact matching, on an ordinary processor,

211
00:14:00,420 --> 00:14:04,266
takes about a hundred microseconds per round at distance nine.

212
00:14:04,266 --> 00:14:06,959
Union-find, a faster decoder, takes about four.

213
00:14:06,959 --> 00:14:09,651
Both are too slow for the clock.

214
00:14:10,346 --> 00:14:13,807
When the decoder falls behind, unprocessed rounds pile up.

215
00:14:13,807 --> 00:14:16,500
Some logical operations, like the T gate,

216
00:14:16,500 --> 00:14:19,961
must wait for the decoder before they can continue.

217
00:14:19,961 --> 00:14:22,653
The backlog grows, and the computer stalls.

218
00:14:23,346 --> 00:14:27,192
So error correction is not only a question of accuracy.

219
00:14:27,192 --> 00:14:31,807
A decoder that is right, but too slow, still stops the computer.

220
00:14:31,807 --> 00:14:35,269
That is why real machines need custom decoding hardware,

221
00:14:35,269 --> 00:14:38,346
fast enough to keep pace with the qubits.

222
00:14:39,044 --> 00:14:44,044
Shor's algorithm can break today's encryption, but only on qubits kept below threshold,

223
00:14:44,044 --> 00:14:45,968
with decoders that keep up.

224
00:14:45,968 --> 00:14:47,121
Try it yourself:

225
00:14:47,121 --> 00:14:52,121
factor fifteen, play the game, race the decoder, and watch the threshold appear.
